# Baaki — Resume Positioning Guide (v2, scoped)

> Supersedes the v1 24-phase plan below this line only in *scope* — the v1
> draft is kept as a record of what was considered and deliberately cut.
> This version is written to be actually finishable by one person, and to
> survive a skeptical 45-minute interviewer at a top product company,
> rather than to maximize the number of technologies listed.

## The one rule everything else follows

`docs/expense-settlement-app-tech-spec.md` §2/§7/§8 already state the
project's real thesis: the skill being tested is knowing **when**
complexity is warranted, not defaulting to maximum complexity. A resume
project that lists more technologies than its owner can defend for 20
minutes each reads as buzzword-stuffing to anyone who has actually
operated production systems — and that's exactly who interviews at top
PBCs/MNCs. v1 of this doc (Kafka + Redis + S3 + Elasticsearch + full OAuth
+ CQRS + Saga + sharding + canary + circuit breakers, all for a project
with no real traffic) violated that rule everywhere. This version doesn't.

Depth on 3-4 axes, each with a real story and real numbers, beats breadth
across 20 buzzwords every time an interviewer actually probes.

Real users are explicitly **not** in scope — this is a portfolio piece,
not a product launch. That changes Week 5's goal from "deploy + onboard
real users" to "deploy to a reachable URL, demoed with seed data."

## Where the project already stands

This is more than it looks like from the outside — don't undersell it in
an interview:

- **Ledger design + all 4 split types**, rounding-safe, with the
  deterministic remainder rule, fully tested (`SplitCalculatorTest`).
- **Greedy debt-simplification** (two-heap, O(n log n), ≤ n−1
  transactions), tested against the adversarial cases (cycles, mega-debtor,
  everyone-owes-everyone) plus a full end-to-end 4-person scenario through
  the real API.
- **Idempotency done properly**: unique-constraint race handling, a true
  concurrent-duplicate-request test (not just sequential retry), and a
  real bug found and fixed — idempotency keys are globally unique but
  weren't checked against the requesting group, so key reuse across groups
  silently returned the wrong group's settlement.
- **Optimistic locking with retry** on the balance cache, plus (new) an
  actual cache **read-through path** with a correctness-verified fallback
  for cold/partial caches — the cache existed for a while as pure
  write-only dead weight before this was wired up. That "found it was
  dead weight, fixed the read path, verified the fix with tests" story is
  itself a good interview answer.
- **Real, measured performance work**: `docs/benchmarks.md` — FK indexing
  (4.4x), cache read-through on top of that (42x), against a dedicated
  650k-row load-test dataset kept isolated from both the demo data and the
  test suite. Numbers weren't invented; they're `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` output
  against production queries. Keep doing this — see the note on Redis below.
- **Observability that's actually wired**: OpenTelemetry traces → Zipkin,
  Prometheus metrics via Micrometer, structured JSON logs — not just
  present in a dependency list.
- **56 backend tests** covering unit, algorithm-adversarial, and full
  Testcontainers-backed integration levels, including membership-boundary
  and cross-group edge cases most CRUD demos never touch.
- A functioning frontend with real seed data, realistic enough to demo
  cold in an interview without narrating around empty screens.

## What's actually left, in priority order

Do these in order — each one is cheap relative to its interview value,
and the order front-loads the highest leverage-per-hour work.

**1. Write the README.** This is the single highest-leverage remaining
task and it's almost entirely writing, not building. It needs: what this
is and isn't (open with the §2 monolith-deliberately framing), the
architecture diagram, the §7 trade-offs table verbatim (ledger vs.
mutable balance, greedy vs. optimal debt simplification, sync vs. async
settlement, monolith vs. microservices, SQL vs. NoSQL), the
`docs/benchmarks.md` numbers folded in or linked, how to run it locally,
and the live demo link once step 2 is done. This is the artifact an
interviewer actually reads before the call — it does half the selling for
you before you say a word.

**2. Deploy it.** Railway or Render (free tier) for the app, a managed
Postgres (Neon/Supabase), Redis Cloud free tier if Redis survives step 4.
Per tech-spec §11. No real-user onboarding — the seed data *is* the demo.
Smoke-test every tab after deploy (cold start, migrations applying
cleanly against a fresh managed Postgres is itself worth verifying, not
assuming).

**3. Kafka notification service — scoped exactly as tech-spec §14, not
wider.** One topic (`ledger-events`, partitioned by `groupId`), `AFTER_COMMIT`
publish, a genuinely separate small consumer service, idempotent
consumption via a `processed_events` table, a dead-letter topic. This is
the one piece of real distributed-systems story currently missing, and
it's pre-justified with a real reason (notifications are fire-and-forget,
tolerant of async decoupling — unlike the ledger writes, which stay
synchronous on purpose). Resist the urge to add more event types or
consumers than the notification use case actually needs.

**4. Decide Redis's fate — don't leave it running unused.** Right now
it's provisioned in `docker-compose.yml` with zero application code
touching it. An interviewer who asks "walk me through your Redis usage"
and gets "it's not actually used" is a bad moment to have manufactured
for yourself. Two legitimate options, pick one:
   - **Use it narrowly for something Postgres doesn't already cover**:
     a Redis-backed fast-path check on the idempotency key (avoid a DB
     round-trip for the common "client retried the same request" case)
     is the one addition that directly extends the idempotency story
     already built, rather than bolting on something unrelated.
   - **Remove it and say so in the README.** "Considered Redis for the
     balance cache; Postgres's `balance_cache` table already served
     that need with a simpler operational footprint, so I didn't add
     infra for infra's sake" is a *stronger* answer than a token Redis
     integration — it's the same engineering-judgment story the whole
     project is built around, applied to itself.

**5. (Optional, do last, only if time remains) Minimal real auth.**
The fake/local-storage auth is explicitly fine per tech-spec §1 ("keep
auth minimal — this isn't the point of the project"), and a full
register/login/JWT/refresh-rotation/OAuth/email-verification/
account-locking stack (v1's Phase 2) adds a large surface area to get
right for a topic this project isn't actually about. If there's spare
time after 1-4, a bare JWT login (no refresh rotation, no OAuth, no email
flows) replacing the fake auth is a reasonable bounded addition. It is
not worth delaying deployment or the README for.

## Explicit non-goals — put these in the README's "Future Improvements," never build them

S3/receipt storage, Elasticsearch/search, full OAuth + email-verification +
account-locking, CQRS, Saga, read replicas, sharding, feature flags,
blue-green/canary deployment, distributed locking, event replay,
snapshotting, materialized views, circuit breakers, bulkheads, AI
features (OCR, NL expense entry, auto-categorization). Naming these as
*reasoned decisions not to build*, with one sentence each on why and what
would change at 10x/100x scale, is worth more in an interview than a
shallow attempt at any one of them — it's the same "why NOT X" judgment
tech-spec §2 already argues is the actual skill being tested.

## What this unlocks in an interview (only the parts that are real)

| Topic | Where it lives |
|---|---|
| Ledger design, concurrency-safety by construction | tech-spec §3, §7 |
| Idempotency + the cross-group key bug you found and fixed | `SettlementService`, `ExpenseSettlementServiceApplicationIT` |
| Optimistic locking + cache read-through correctness | `BalanceCacheService`, `BalanceService`, `docs/benchmarks.md` |
| Greedy algorithm design + NP-hardness reasoning | `DebtSimplifier`, tech-spec §5.3 |
| Measured performance work (not invented numbers) | `docs/benchmarks.md` |
| Observability | Zipkin traces, Prometheus metrics, structured logs |
| Event-driven design, once §14's Kafka piece lands | tech-spec §14 |
| Judgment about what *not* to build | this doc's non-goals list, README's Future Improvements |

Every row above should be answerable from actual implementation, not
theory — that's the bar, and right now most of this table already clears
it.

